          "secNumber": "1.3",
          "heading": "Advanced investigation: cell mechanics, tissue origin, and vascular identity",
          "content": "### Water potential is a balance of chemical and mechanical forces\n\nFor a dilute aqueous compartment, plant physiologists commonly express water potential as the sum of solute potential and pressure potential, with gravitational and matric terms added when their magnitude matters: \u03a8w = \u03a8s + \u03a8p (+ \u03a8g + \u03a8m). All terms are pressures, typically reported in megapascals (MPa). Pure free water at standard reference conditions has \u03a8w = 0 MPa. Dissolved solute generally makes \u03a8s negative, whereas positive turgor raises \u03a8p. Water moves spontaneously from a region of higher water potential to a region of lower water potential, provided a conductive pathway exists. A membrane can select among solutes while permitting water flow, so external and internal osmotic conditions must not be treated as interchangeable.\n\nImagine a living cell with \u03a8s = \u22120.80 MPa and \u03a8p = +0.50 MPa. Its approximate \u03a8w is \u22120.30 MPa. If the surrounding solution has \u03a8w = \u22120.45 MPa, net water tends to leave this cell initially; the process changes volume and turgor, so the starting numbers are not a permanent prediction. By contrast a cell with a thick secondary wall but no living protoplast cannot maintain metabolic solute gradients or turgor the way a living parenchyma cell can. This is why the mechanical contributions of cellulose, lignin, and hydrostatic pressure must be distinguished.\n\n### A developmental lineage is not a static anatomical label\n\nA shoot apical meristem maintains an organized stem-cell domain and produces lateral organ primordia. Root apical meristems maintain zones of cell division, elongation, and differentiation. Cambial activity increases the girth of stems and roots through secondary xylem and phloem production. These processes share cell division but differ in geometry, positional information, and tissue outputs. A transverse stem section may reveal xylem vessel elements and phloem sieve elements; it does not directly reveal the time sequence in which they differentiated. Histology, pulse labeling, and developmental series answer different questions.\n\nPlasmodesmata connect adjacent living plant cells through their walls, forming symplastic routes for selected molecules. Apoplastic pathways run through cell walls and extracellular spaces. The Casparian strip in the root endodermis can restrict unrestricted apoplastic access to the stele, forcing more selective membrane crossing. Neither the symplast nor the apoplast should be drawn as an unconditional uninterrupted tube: developmental state, plasmodesmatal gating, and suberization alter passage.\n\n### Quantitative case: osmotic potential of a teaching solution\n\nThe van 't Hoff approximation \u03a8s \u2248 \u2212iCRT is appropriate only for sufficiently dilute ideal solutions. For a hypothetical 0.20 mol L\u207b\u00b9 non-dissociating solute at 298 K, with R = 0.008314 L MPa mol\u207b\u00b9 K\u207b\u00b9, calculate \u03a8s \u2248 \u2212(1)(0.20)(0.008314)(298) = \u22120.496 MPa. A living cell at \u03a8p = +0.30 MPa with the same approximate internal solute potential would have \u03a8w \u2248 \u22120.196 MPa. The two calculations deliberately answer different questions. Real cellular sap contains mixed nonideal solutes and compartment-specific concentrations; osmometry or pressure measurements are required for empirical values.\n\n**Experimental challenge.** Compare epidermal peels from leaves exposed to two controlled osmotic conditions. Blind-score cell outline and membrane displacement, include biological replicates from independently grown plants, and record the elapsed time since transfer. Explain why multiple microscope images from one leaf do not constitute multiple independent plants. Test whether measured plasmolysis thresholds align with the ideal model, and identify uncertainties from solute permeability and cell-wall geometry.\n\n**Diagnostic questions.** Why can xylem vessels transport water after programmed cell death? Which experiment distinguishes wall stiffness from turgor? How would you demonstrate that a young tissue differentiates into xylem rather than merely resembling it in a single section?",
