          "heading": "Advanced investigation: anatomy as a functional system",
          "content": "### Roots exhibit radial organization and changing permeability\n\nA root is not simply a water-absorbing pipe. The epidermis, cortex, endodermis, pericycle, and vascular cylinder have different anatomical roles; their relative widths and degrees of suberization vary with root age and species. Young root hairs increase the effective area of contact with soil microsites, but their abundance alone does not guarantee maximal water acquisition when soil hydraulic conductivity is limiting. Lateral roots typically originate from internal tissue associated with the pericycle, unlike shoot branches, which originate from meristems in shoot tissues. This contrast is especially useful when interpreting plant architecture.\n\nWater delivery to the root surface and membrane uptake are distinct resistances. In drying soil, the steepest hydraulic gradient can occur outside the root, and apparent uptake capacity may reflect soil-root contact rather than aquaporin activity. Root cross sections at several distances from the tip can reveal maturation gradients. A single section is insufficient evidence for the permeability of an entire root system.\n\n### Leaf trade-offs connect geometry to gas exchange\n\nLeaves must gather light while exchanging carbon dioxide with the atmosphere and retaining water. A thick mesophyll increases photosynthetic tissue but also extends internal diffusion paths; stomatal opening increases CO\u2082 access while increasing water-vapor exchange under most conditions. The boundary layer, internal leaf air spaces, cuticle, and stomata each contribute different transport constraints. Sun-grown and shade-grown leaves can differ in thickness, leaf mass per area, and pigment investment. These are plastic responses, not universal species rankings.\n\nThe stomatal density of a leaf is stomata counted per measured leaf area, whereas stomatal index is stomata divided by stomata plus ordinary epidermal cells, usually expressed as a percentage. They are related but nonidentical. Both should be sampled from comparable developmental positions because epidermal expansion affects apparent density.\n\n### Quantitative case: specific leaf area and sampling\n\nSpecific leaf area (SLA) is leaf area divided by dry mass. Suppose three leaves from a hypothetical plant have respective measured areas of 24, 28, and 26 cm\u00b2 and dry masses of 0.12, 0.14, and 0.13 g. Each gives an SLA of 200 cm\u00b2 g\u207b\u00b9; the area-weighted aggregate is (24 + 28 + 26)/(0.12 + 0.14 + 0.13) = 78/0.39 = 200 cm\u00b2 g\u207b\u00b9. If one leaf were partially desiccated before area measurement, the result would not be directly comparable without a consistent sampling protocol. Dry mass must be determined by a documented drying procedure, not confused with fresh mass.\n\n**Field protocol.** Select independently growing individuals within comparable light regimes; sample the same leaf developmental category; image blades beside a scale; measure area using a calibrated segmentation procedure; dry to stable mass under a specified protocol. Report the number of plants and leaves separately. Analyze between-plant variation instead of presenting multiple leaves from one plant as independent replicates.\n\n**Mechanism check.** Predict what would happen to measured stomatal density if epidermal cells expanded after stomatal initiation but no new stomata formed. Explain why roots in waterlogged soil may experience oxygen shortage despite abundant water. Distinguish lateral-root initiation from root-hair elongation.",
