          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Quantitative research clinic: Organ anatomy and sampling across developmental gradients",
          "content": "A leaf cross-section is not a random sample of an entire plant. Midrib tissue, the lamina margin and the central lamina differ in vascular density, mesophyll arrangement and mechanical support. A comparison of leaf thickness from two habitats must therefore standardize species, leaf developmental stage, orientation and sampled position before attributing differences to the habitat. Plant responses are often plastic: a thicker leaf can reflect light acclimation, water availability or genotype.\n\n**Worked sampling example.** Suppose five independent plants from each of two treatments contribute one mature leaf each. The five mean lamina thickness values in micrometres are A: 182, 190, 178, 194, 186 and B: 210, 205, 217, 209, 214. The treatment means are 186 and 211 micrometres, an illustrative difference of 25 micrometres. Measuring ten microscopic fields in each leaf improves estimation of a leaf's thickness but does not turn the study into 50 independent plants per group. Report between-plant variability and avoid inferring causality if plants were collected opportunistically from separate sites.\n\n**Mechanism question.** A root cross-section showing a larger cortex is not automatically evidence of greater nutrient uptake. Nutrient flux depends on concentration gradients, active transport, mycorrhizal relationships, surface area, water movement and tissue viability. Root hairs change absorptive area, while endodermal barriers constrain apoplastic movement into the stele. Distinguish anatomy that enables a process from measurements that demonstrate its rate.\n\n**Field-to-lab workflow.** Label each whole plant before excision; record root order, organ age and the exact sectioning location. Fixation and dehydration may distort cell dimensions, so record preparation methods and compare like with like. Use scale calibration within each microscope session and include a biological reference structure if possible. An effective interpretation also anticipates tissue orientation: an oblique cut through a cylinder can overestimate its apparent diameter. This geometric artifact is measurable, unlike a vague impression that a specimen simply looks larger.",
