          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Quantitative research clinic: Hydraulic conductance and nutrient deficiency diagnosis",
          "content": "Water transport through the soil\u2013plant\u2013atmosphere continuum is driven by differences in water potential, although living tissues add regulation and complex resistances. For an introductory analogy, write flux J = K \u00d7 \u0394\u03a8, where K is an effective conductance under specified conditions. This relation is local and conditional: conductivity can change with embolism, aquaporin activity, temperature and xylem anatomy. Do not assume the same K applies across severe drought and well-watered regimes.\n\n**Worked calculation.** In a hypothetical experimental stem, water flux is 2.4 mmol s\u22121 at an imposed potential difference of 0.60 MPa. The inferred effective conductance is 4.0 mmol s\u22121 MPa\u22121. If the pressure difference rises to 0.90 MPa, a naive linear extrapolation predicts 3.6 mmol s\u22121. A measured value of 2.7 mmol s\u22121 would therefore challenge the constant-conductance assumption; it would not independently identify embolism without supporting measurements. Check that the sign convention and organ area normalization are reported.\n\nMineral nutrition creates an additional diagnostic problem. Interveinal chlorosis may arise from nutrient availability, uptake limitations, root stress or pathogens. The mobility of a nutrient can inform which leaves first show symptoms, but symptom location is only one part of an evidence chain. A controlled deficiency study needs nutrient-sufficient controls, nutrient-solution chemistry, pH monitoring and independent plants. Confounding arises when replacing a salt to remove one nutrient inadvertently changes counter-ions or solution osmotic strength.\n\n**Discriminating test.** Compare the effect of restoring the suspected nutrient on new growth, while assessing tissue nutrient concentrations and root health where feasible. Do not interpret rapid greening of old tissue as a universal response; mobility and developmental stage matter. Separate substrate concentration, tissue concentration and growth rate in reporting. A concentration can appear high in stunted plants because biomass accumulation slowed, a dilution-effect reversal that does not establish excess uptake.",
