          "secNumber": "5.3",
          "heading": "Advanced investigation: transport pathways and source\u2013sink dynamics",
          "content": "### The physical nature of xylem and phloem flow differs\n\nXylem water moves through dead conductive tracheary elements under substantial negative pressure in many transpiring plants. Phloem carries sugars, amino acids, signaling compounds, and other solutes through living sieve elements connected to metabolically active tissues. In a pressure-flow account, loading near a source increases local phloem solute concentration, water enters from adjacent xylem, and the resulting pressure difference supports bulk transport toward lower-pressure regions where unloading occurs. Actual loading and unloading mechanisms vary across species and organs.\n\nA developing fruit, actively growing root, or filling seed may be a strong sink; a photosynthetically mature leaf often functions as a source. Source and sink are functional roles rather than immutable organ labels. A young leaf can be a sink before it becomes a net exporter. A storage root can accumulate reserves in one season and mobilize them later. Girdling can help test the consequences of interrupting phloem in a stem, but it also injures tissue and can indirectly alter water and hormone relationships, so it is not a perfectly selective perturbation.\n\n### Isotope tracing requires disciplined inference\n\nPulse-label experiments can track the movement of labeled assimilates through a plant. Label appearance in an organ reflects a mixture of delivery, retention, metabolism, and pool dilution. A strong terminal label signal does not by itself demonstrate a higher instantaneous transport velocity. Sampling multiple times, normalizing to tissue mass, and accounting for initial source labeling improve interpretation. When the source organ changes its photosynthetic rate, source loading becomes a confounder.\n\n### Quantitative case: mass transfer rather than mere concentration\n\nA hypothetical stem phloem sample has estimated sucrose concentration 0.50 mol L\u207b\u00b9 and sap volume flow rate 2.0 \u00b5L min\u207b\u00b9. If concentration and flow are approximately uniform, sucrose molar delivery is (0.50 mol L\u207b\u00b9)(2.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 L min\u207b\u00b9) = 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 mol min\u207b\u00b9 = 1.0 \u00b5mol min\u207b\u00b9. With sucrose molar mass near 342 g mol\u207b\u00b9, mass delivery is 0.342 mg min\u207b\u00b9. Concentration alone cannot establish a delivery rate; without independent velocity or flow-area information, mass flux is unknown. These numbers are illustrative, not direct field measurements.\n\n**Study design.** Grow plants under matched conditions, define which organs are sources and sinks at the sampling stage, conduct a short labeled-carbon pulse where appropriate safety and facilities exist, and collect time-series observations from multiple biological individuals. Include untreated or sham-handled controls. Quantify source-specific labeling and tissue mass, and avoid confusing per-organ accumulation with fraction of total recovered label.\n\n**Diagnostic questions.** Why is high sugar concentration not sufficient evidence of rapid export? What changes when a sink becomes a source? Which alternative effect complicates girdling interpretation? Why can phloem sap travel in different directions in different vascular bundles without violating the pressure-flow concept?",
