          "content": "### Plant hormones are context-dependent regulators\n\nAuxin patterns and polarized transport contribute to axis formation, tropisms, and organ development, but auxin concentration alone cannot predict the response of every tissue. Cytokinins often influence cell division and shoot-related developmental programs. Gibberellins can promote elongation and aspects of germination; abscisic acid participates in dormancy and drought responses; ethylene can regulate ripening and stress-associated development. These summaries are not one-hormone\u2013one-effect rules. Tissue sensitivity, receptor expression, cross-talk, and developmental age alter outcomes.\n\nPhototropism occurs when asymmetric growth redirects a shoot toward the light under appropriate conditions; it is not necessarily due to one side receiving more total energy for photosynthesis. Gravitropism involves directional sensing and altered growth in roots or shoots. Time-lapse image analysis can measure curvature, but a curvature angle is distinct from elongation rate. A short hypocotyl under one light quality cannot independently identify the concentration of a particular hormone.\n\n### Development connects genetic programs to environment\n\nPhotoperiodic flowering pathways respond to the relative timing of light and darkness through circadian-regulated perception in many species. Vernalization is a prolonged cold-associated acquisition of flowering competence in certain plants. Some species require one cue, both cues, or neither. A plant's flowering date also depends on age, nutrition, temperature, and genotype; classifying all plants as strictly short-day or long-day ignores nonphotoperiodic forms and quantitative responses.\n\n### Quantitative case: relative growth rate\n\nA hypothetical group of independent plants has mean dry mass of 2.0 g at the start and 3.2 g after 14 days. Under an exponential-growth approximation, relative growth rate RGR = [ln(3.2) \u2212 ln(2.0)]/14 = ln(1.6)/14 \u2248 0.0336 day\u207b\u00b9. If the plants show delayed or sigmoidal growth, one interval cannot establish a constant RGR. Since destructive harvests cannot measure the same plant's dry mass twice, independent randomized cohorts may be necessary and uncertainty must be derived from replicate data.\n\n**Experimental design.** Randomize seedlings among control and light-direction treatments; quantify stem curvature from standardized time-lapse images; independently record stem extension, photon flux, and temperature. For flowering, use a factorial photoperiod-by-temperature design while holding seedling age constant. Measure time to flowering and proportion flowering rather than collapsing both outcomes into a single ambiguous 'growth score.'\n\n**Diagnostic questions.** Why can the same auxin treatment stimulate one organ and inhibit another? What distinguishes dormancy release from seed viability? How can a circadian clock cause different responses to equal-duration illumination delivered at different times? Why does final height fail to identify the mechanism controlling elongation?",
