          "secNumber": "6.6",
          "heading": "Quantitative research clinic: Hormone interactions and factorial design",
          "content": "Plant hormones are signals in networks, not independent switches. The direction and size of an auxin response depend on tissue, dose, developmental state and interaction with other signals. Gibberellins can promote growth in some contexts, while abscisic acid is important for drought responses and dormancy; neither hormone should be described as universally activating or suppressing all growth processes. A factorial experiment is useful because it can reveal interactions missed by one-factor-at-a-time testing.\n\n**Worked two-factor design.** Suppose mean hypocotyl length in a teaching dataset is 8 mm without either treatment, 11 mm with treatment A alone, 9 mm with B alone and 15 mm with both. The additive prediction for the combined treatment is 8+(11\u22128)+(9\u22128)=12 mm. The observed combined mean is 3 mm above this additive prediction. That arithmetic describes an interaction contrast, but whether the effect is distinguishable from noise depends on biological replicate variance and design. Do not infer a receptor-level mechanism from four means.\n\n**Experimental controls.** Deliver solvent-only controls, document application position and concentration, and account for unequal germination or initial seedling size. Dose\u2013response relationships can be nonlinear, so interpreting two doses as a universal trend is risky. Randomize pot positions or rotate them when directional illumination creates gradients. If all treated seedlings occupy one tray, tray environment may be confounded with the hormone treatment. Biological independence belongs to the unit that received the intervention.\n\n**Mechanistic follow-up.** Compare the phenotype in two distinct tissues, sample transcript or reporter responses when appropriate, and test whether a response persists if another pathway is perturbed. Temporal resolution matters because gene-expression changes can precede measurable elongation. A useful lab report explicitly states which data show an association, which show an interaction, and which additional interventions would be required to identify pathway order.",
