          "secNumber": "7.3",
          "heading": "Advanced investigation: plant reproduction, dispersal, and evolutionary interpretation",
          "content": "### Alternation of generations clarifies the meaning of gametes and spores\n\nLand-plant life cycles alternate multicellular diploid sporophyte and multicellular haploid gametophyte phases. Meiosis in the sporophyte produces haploid spores; mitosis in the gametophyte produces gametes. Fertilization restores diploidy. In flowering plants, the gametophytes are highly reduced and depend on sporophytic tissues for much of their development. This is why calling pollen a 'male gamete' is inaccurate: the pollen grain is a male gametophyte, which generates or carries sperm cells depending on developmental stage.\n\nDouble fertilization in angiosperms usually yields a diploid embryo and a typically triploid endosperm, although endosperm ploidy varies among some lineages. Pollination moves pollen to a receptive structure; fertilization occurs later and may fail even after apparently successful pollination. Fruit formation, seed viability, and germination are sequential outcomes with different failure points. Seed dormancy is not synonymous with seed death, and a viable seed can remain ungerminated under unsuitable conditions.\n\n### Breeding systems alter genetic interpretation\n\nSelf-compatibility, self-incompatibility, floral timing, pollinator access, and clonal reproduction influence realized mating patterns. A plant that possesses both male and female floral structures is not automatically self-fertilizing. In population studies, low seed set can reflect pollen limitation, resource limitation, herbivory, incompatibility, or abortion of developing fruits. An experiment adding compatible pollen should include sham-handled controls and must record whether pollination manipulations themselves damage stigmas or flowers.\n\n### Quantitative case: germination and experimental units\n\nFour independently prepared seed trays each receive 50 viable-looking seeds. Germination counts are 41, 44, 39, and 46 after a prespecified 10-day assessment, giving rates of 82%, 88%, 78%, and 92%. Their pooled proportion is 170/200 = 85%. Reporting only 200 seeds as 200 independent experimental units overstates independent replication if a common tray environment is influential. The tray-level mean is also 85%, but between-tray variability matters. Report the precise germination criterion (for example radicle emergence) and verify viability separately for ungerminated seeds where appropriate.\n\n**Experimental design.** Compare treatments across randomly positioned replicate trays with equal seed source, storage history, substrate, moisture, and temperature. Record daily cumulative germination curves rather than only the final fraction, because equal final germination can mask different rates. To study pollen limitation, assign flowers across independent plants to supplemental cross-pollen, sham contact, and unmanipulated conditions and measure fruit as well as seed set.\n\n**Diagnostic questions.** What does meiosis produce in a flowering plant life cycle? Why does pollen transfer not guarantee seed development? How could a seed be alive without germinating? Explain why flowering time can affect gene flow even when two species have overlapping geographic ranges.",
