          "heading": "Practicum 7: Plant responses to environmental stress",
          "content": "### The question and why it matters\n\n**System:** Salinity acclimation in two genotypes. The central aim of this investigation is to move from a descriptive observation to a reproducible biological measurement. Experimental interpretations are strongest when the instrument output, the biological quantity of interest, and the causal claim are kept distinct. A quantitative value is useful only with its definition, units, assumptions and uncertainty. Many textbook diagrams intentionally omit these constraints; laboratory investigation is where those omissions become visible.\n\n### Experimental design\n\nCompare a salt-sensitive and a salt-tolerant genotype in a randomized factorial experiment. Increase salt gradually to avoid confusing osmotic shock with longer-term ionic toxicity. Match pots for substrate and irrigation regime. Record electrical conductivity of drainage solution rather than assuming target salinity equals root-zone exposure. Measure shoot and root dry mass, leaf water status and visible damage at several time points. Control for differential growth rate before treatment and beware leaf-area normalization that can hide actual plant productivity losses.\n\nBefore collecting data, write a hypothesis with a directional prediction, define an observation window, and select a primary outcome. Predefine exclusion criteria for damaged material and log departures from the protocol. Plan at least three truly independent biological replicates for a small teaching pilot, while recognizing that a formal sample-size calculation depends on expected effect and variability. Random allocation mitigates positional bias but cannot fix systematic instrument calibration error. Mask sample identifiers during image scoring if practical; if blinding is impossible, use automated measurements and a predeclared scoring rubric to reduce subjective decisions.\n\n### Worked quantitative interpretation\n\nIn an illustrative dataset, genotype A declines from 5.0 g to 3.5 g shoot dry mass under salinity, while genotype B declines from 4.0 g to 3.4 g. Relative retention is 70% and 85% respectively. Despite better retention, B has lower absolute stressed mass than A (3.4 versus 3.5 g). \u201cTolerance\u201d must therefore be operationally defined before declaring a winner.\n\nA reproducible report should retain raw readings alongside any normalized value. If repeated values appear, show their distribution and the source of variability before constructing confidence intervals. Converting a measured number into a larger biological conclusion requires an explicit assumption chain. For example, a bulk gravimetric measurement does not resolve cell-level transport; a count of responsive cells does not measure pathway activation; an averaged population response does not characterize every individual. These distinctions prevent overstating the meaning of the calculated estimate.\n\n### Mistake to identify and repair\n\nCompatible solutes necessarily cause salt tolerance: correlation is not proof. Stress can induce metabolites as damage markers as well as protective responses. Manipulation of synthesis or transport is needed to test causal contributions.\n\nThe repair is to formulate two competing explanations and state a discriminating measurement. Draw a causal graph with the manipulated exposure, the proposed mediator, the measured endpoint, and at least one confounder. Separating a mechanistic hypothesis from its observable consequences is essential because several mechanisms can predict the same direction of change. When no available measurement separates the explanations, report the result as descriptive rather than mechanistically decisive.\n\n### Data record and quality controls\n\nRecord a unique replicate identifier; species, genotype and source where applicable; pretreatment growth conditions; measurement date; treatment assignment; instrument resolution; sample handling; and the raw endpoint. Use SI-compatible units and explicitly document any conversion. If a balance reads to 0.01 g, reporting a derived mass to six decimal places falsely suggests precision. Verify blank controls, out-of-range samples, and response saturation before averaging. If an instrument drifts over a session, record standards at both the beginning and end instead of simply assuming constancy. Archive sample exclusions with reasons, including exclusions made after seeing a result.\n\nDistinguish **technical replication**, where one biological sample is measured repeatedly, from **biological replication**, where independently sampled individuals, populations, or experimental units are compared. Use the structure of the intervention to identify the correct unit for statistical analysis: if an entire container receives the treatment, the container is not interchangeable with each plant inside it. A design with many observations but few independent treated units can produce precise-looking yet misleading uncertainty estimates.\n\n### Extension challenge\n\nChoose a primary outcome for a breeding program (absolute stressed biomass or proportional retention), justify it, and discuss how that choice changes genotype ranking.\n\nWrite a short report containing (1) a falsifiable hypothesis; (2) a labelled experimental diagram; (3) a raw-data table; (4) the result with correct units and one numerical uncertainty statement where replicate data allow it; (5) an alternative mechanism and a proposed follow-up test; and (6) a clear boundary between classroom illustration and generalizable biological inference. Include a paragraph on experimental ethics and local biosafety: ordinary plant work is low risk in many settings, but field collection permits, invasive species controls, nutrient-waste disposal and institutional guidance still matter.",
