          "secNumber": "8.3",
          "heading": "Advanced investigation: plant stress, community processes, and reproducible inquiry",
          "content": "### Stress experiments require separating exposure from response\n\nA drought treatment can be described by soil water content, soil water potential, fraction of transpirable soil water, or duration without irrigation. These quantities are not synonymous; pot size, evaporative demand, and rooting depth change the meaning of each. Physiological responses may include stomatal closure, osmotic adjustment, altered growth allocation, and changes in hydraulic vulnerability. Their timing can differ: rapid stomatal responses do not prove long-term drought adaptation. A genotype that survives a short dry-down in a small pot is not necessarily superior in field yield.\n\nSalinity can impose both osmotic stress and ion-specific toxicity. Heat stress can change enzyme function, membrane stability, and reproductive development. Shade can alter leaf area, pigment composition, and stem architecture. Stress combinations can produce nonadditive effects, so one-factor-at-a-time results should not be extrapolated uncritically to simultaneous drought and heat.\n\n### Communities connect individual performance and biodiversity\n\nPlants compete for light, water, nutrients, and space, but they also modify each other's microenvironments. A canopy can reduce light and soil evaporation, while roots and litter alter soil structure and nutrient cycling. Trait differences influence interactions yet do not support simplistic labels such as 'fast-growing species always outcompete slow-growing species.' Ecosystem productivity, local species abundance, and carbon storage are distinct metrics. Aboveground biomass inventories miss belowground pools, mortality, decomposition, and soil carbon changes.\n\n### Quantitative case: paired treatment effect\n\nIn a hypothetical randomized block experiment, four blocks each contain a control and a salinity-treated plant. Control shoot dry masses are 8.0, 9.1, 7.5, and 8.4 g; treated masses are 6.8, 7.7, 6.4, and 7.0 g. Blockwise treatment-minus-control differences are \u22121.2, \u22121.4, \u22121.1, and \u22121.4 g, averaging \u22121.275 g. A percent reduction computed from treatment and control means is [(8.25 \u2212 6.975)/8.25] \u00d7 100 \u2248 15.45%. Because four block pairs, not eight unrelated observations, define the randomized comparisons, statistical inference should preserve blocking. Neither the mean difference nor percentage alone provides a confidence interval.\n\n**Research workflow.** Register the hypothesis, unit of randomization, outcomes, exclusions, and primary analysis before collecting data. Calibrate soil-moisture sensors and report the depth and location of measurements. Record unexpected mortality and missing data rather than quietly deleting plants. Share treatment definitions, analysis code, deidentified raw measurements, units, and software versions. Explain whether a reported result supports causal inference from randomized treatment or merely an observational association.\n\n**Diagnostic questions.** Why does withholding the same volume of water impose unequal stress on differently sized plants? How might a drought-tolerant species have low growth under abundant water? Why is reduced plant height not sufficient evidence of lower photosynthesis? Which metadata would make a field study reproducible at another site?",
